  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The engagement</div>
      <h2>A capable services team needed a pipeline beyond referrals and personal networks.</h2>
      <p class="cs2-intro">The confidential provider sold expert outsourced services to businesses. Its people could scope and deliver the work once a legitimate need was clear. The challenge was finding organizations with a plausible capacity or operating problem and starting the conversation without sounding like another generic consultancy.</p>
      <div class="cs2-three">
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Company</span><h3>Professional services provider</h3><p>A confidential B2B firm delivering expert outsourced support around defined business work and outcomes.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Target market</span><h3>Businesses with a capacity decision</h3><p>Organizations where growth, workload, hiring, change, or a project could create a reason to compare internal and external delivery.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Primary outcome</span><h3>Qualified commercial conversations</h3><p>Meetings where a responsible buyer, real work, plausible delivery fit, and defined next-step purpose were established.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The commercial problem</div>
      <h2>Professional services are bought through trust, timing, and operating fit—not feature comparison.</h2>
      <p class="cs2-intro">The offer was valuable but intangible. Buyers needed to understand what work an external team would own, how quality and control would operate, and why a conversation was useful now. Generic claims about expertise could not answer those questions.</p>
      <div class="cs2-three">
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">01 · Trust</span><h3>Proof is contextual</h3><p>Experience only mattered when connected to the buyer's work, risk, service standard, and decision.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">02 · Alternatives</span><h3>Hire, incumbent, or delay</h3><p>The provider competed with internal hiring, existing partners, temporary workarounds, and the option to do nothing.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">03 · Timing</span><h3>Need changes quickly</h3><p>Growth, backlog, leadership, projects, and capacity could make the offer relevant, but only a buyer could confirm the pressure.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="cs2-truth">The call did not try to prove outsourcing was always better. It investigated whether a bridge, specialist, overflow, managed, or hybrid model improved the buyer's operating plan.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Target market design</div>
      <h2>The campaign looked for a real delivery decision, not every company with a budget.</h2>
      <p class="cs2-intro">Account research combined company fit, the work the provider could genuinely deliver, likely owners, and observable changes that might make capacity or specialist expertise worth discussing.</p>
      <div class="cs2-six">
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Account fit</span><h3>Suitable operating environment</h3><p>Organizations with the scale, work type, geography, and commercial profile the provider could support.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Buyer roles</span><h3>Finance and operations</h3><p>Leaders accountable for capacity, cost, delivery quality, projects, process ownership, or an external service relationship.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Work scope</span><h3>A defined business requirement</h3><p>The research connected the provider to specific work rather than advertising broad consulting capability.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Signals</span><h3>Change worth investigating</h3><p>Hiring, expansion, acquisitions, leadership moves, workload growth, new projects, and visible operating priorities.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Exclusions</span><h3>No workable delivery fit</h3><p>Wrong scope, buyer, geography, risk profile, timing, or no sensible relationship between the signal and the service.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Call reason</span><h3>An operating question</h3><p>The opening asked how the buyer planned to cover the work and whether outside support deserved comparison.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The outbound system</div>
      <h2>Five connected steps turned a broad service into a specific buyer conversation.</h2>
      <div class="cs2-board">
        <div class="cs2-console-head"><span>CALLTEAM PROFESSIONAL SERVICES PATH</span><strong>MARKET TO OPPORTUNITY</strong></div>
        <div class="cs2-flow">
          <article><small>01</small><strong>Define the deliverable</strong><p>Clarify the work, account profile, buyer, delivery models, proof, exclusions, and meeting standard.</p></article>
          <article><small>02</small><strong>Research change and ownership</strong><p>Identify suitable accounts, likely buyers, relevant events, and the internal-versus-external decision.</p></article>
          <article class="active"><small>03</small><strong>Start a human conversation</strong><p>Test the work, timing, current model, capacity, control concerns, and decision route.</p></article>
          <article><small>04</small><strong>Qualify commercial fit</strong><p>Confirm scope, sponsor, urgency, delivery model, constraints, and an appropriate next step.</p></article>
          <article><small>05</small><strong>Hand off buyer context</strong><p>Give the service team the requirement, current approach, objections, timing, and agreed meeting purpose.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="cs2-loop"><b>↻</b> Live conversations refined the target profile, language, delivery model, objection responses, buyer map, and follow-up priorities.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">CallTeam AI in the campaign</div>
      <h2>The signal created a reason to research. The buyer decided whether a need existed.</h2>
      <p class="cs2-intro">Professional services outreach becomes less credible when public change is treated as inside knowledge. The CallTeam system keeps the difference between an observed event, an outreach hypothesis, and a confirmed buyer problem explicit.</p>
      <div class="cs2-signal-grid">
        <article><span>CallTeam Buyer Signal Radar</span><h3>Prioritize plausible capacity decisions</h3><p>The Radar helped organize account fit, public events, buyer roles, previous engagement, and market context before outreach.</p><ul><li>Hiring for work the provider could support</li><li>Expansion, acquisition, or geographic growth</li><li>Leadership changes and new operating priorities</li><li>Projects, workload, backlog, or transformation activity</li></ul></article>
        <article><span>CallTeam AI GTM + human execution</span><h3>Connect preparation to accountable selling</h3><p><a href="/ai-lead-generation-services/">AI Lead Generation</a> supported account discovery, enrichment, and buyer mapping. <a href="/services/ai-gtm/">CallTeam AI GTM</a> connected the intelligence to campaign workflow, calling, qualification, and learning. Experienced callers verified whether the work, timing, and delivery model were real before advancing a meeting.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The qualification gate</div>
      <h2>A meeting counted only when both the need and the delivery model deserved discussion.</h2>
      <div class="cs2-five cs2-flow cs2-board">
        <article><small>01</small><strong>Company fit</strong><p>The organization matched the provider's defined market, work type, scope, and delivery boundaries.</p></article>
        <article><small>02</small><strong>Responsible buyer</strong><p>The contact owned the work, budget, operating decision, or could route the team correctly.</p></article>
        <article><small>03</small><strong>Defined requirement</strong><p>The conversation established a real task, capacity gap, project, service need, or timing issue.</p></article>
        <article><small>04</small><strong>Delivery fit</strong><p>An external, bridge, specialist, overflow, managed, or hybrid model was plausible.</p></article>
        <article class="active"><small>05</small><strong>Agreed next step</strong><p>The buyer understood why the meeting was scheduled and what decision it should advance.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs2-results">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The 90 day outcome</div>
      <h2>Six meetings created three serious opportunities and one signed client.</h2>
      <p class="cs2-intro">The campaign record was described as six to seven booked meetings, so this case study uses the lower confirmed figure. Three prospects showed strong interest and continued into serious commercial discussions. One became a signed client by the end of the 90 day period.</p>
      <div class="cs2-result-grid">
        <article><span>Qualified meetings</span><strong>6</strong><small>The conservative confirmed count from the six-to-seven meeting campaign record.</small></article>
        <article><span>Serious opportunities</span><strong>3</strong><small>Prospects that wanted to continue discussing the service and delivery fit.</small></article>
        <article><span>Signed client</span><strong>1</strong><small>One qualified opportunity became a new client within the 90 day window.</small></article>
      </div>
      <div class="cs2-evidence"><strong>Evidence boundary:</strong> the client remains confidential. The results describe this campaign's scope and measurement period and should not be interpreted as a guarantee.</div>
