  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Why the campaign worked</div>
      <h2>Six decisions made an intangible service easier to evaluate.</h2>
      <div class="cs2-six">
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Positioning</span><h3>Work before expertise</h3><p>The campaign defined the business work the provider could own before describing credentials.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Targeting</span><h3>Decision before directory</h3><p>Accounts were prioritized for a plausible capacity or delivery decision, not just company size.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Signals</span><h3>Timing before assumption</h3><p>Public changes created a careful question while leaving the buyer free to reject the premise.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Conversation</span><h3>Diagnosis before pitch</h3><p>Human callers explored scope, ownership, current approach, risk, and timing before proposing a meeting.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Qualification</span><h3>Delivery fit before handoff</h3><p>The next step needed a responsible buyer, defined work, plausible model, and commercial purpose.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Follow-up</span><h3>Serious interest before pressure</h3><p>Prospects advanced according to the buying process instead of being forced into premature urgency.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="cs2-confidentiality"><strong>Client confidentiality:</strong> The company name, exact service specialty, and identifying details are withheld. Results are reported conservatively.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

